### 4.2 Orbital predictions are estimates

Pass times, elevations, azimuths, ranges, range rates, Doppler offsets and
satellite positions computed by this program are **estimates** derived from
public two-line element sets through the SGP4/SDP4 model. Their error grows with
the age of the element set, with atmospheric density and with unmodelled
manoeuvres. Measured against a real element set, the along-track error reaches
approximately 46 km at 8 days, 292 km at 15 days and 1.45 Mm at 28 days, which
corresponds to roughly 6, 38 and 188 seconds of pass-time error.

Do not use this program, or any output of it, for:

* navigation, attitude control, antenna autotracking of a safety-critical link,
  or any use where a wrong angle or time can cause injury or damage;
* collision avoidance, conjunction assessment or space-traffic coordination;
* radio licensing examinations, regulatory filings or interference complaints;
* any application requiring certified or precise ephemerides.

Precise orbit determination requires precise ephemerides, which this program
does not use and does not claim to approximate.

### 4.3 Third-party data

Element sets and transceiver data come from Celestrak and SatNOGS. Their
accuracy, availability, completeness and timeliness are outside the control of
this project. A satellite can be re-entered, manoeuvred, renamed or removed from
the catalogue without notice, and the program will keep predicting passes for it
until the data is refreshed.

